   105	
   106	`build.win.verifyUpdateCodeSignature` is `false` while builds are unsigned: with it on,
   107	`app-update.yml` carries that publisher name and electron-updater refuses every unsigned
   108	update. Turn it back on in the first signed release, never before: an installed build with it
   109	on refuses every later update that is not signed by `Useful Build`.
   110	
   111	---
   112	
   113	## Architecture
   114	
   115	```
   116	windows/
   117	  src/
   118	    core/        platform-free TypeScript, no Electron, no Node, no DOM
   119	    main/        Electron main process: key, files, hotkey, delivery
   120	    preload/     the only bridge exposed to the renderer
   121	    renderer/    UI, and the audio capture host
   122	  tests/         vitest suites for src/core and the dictation state machine
   123	  scripts/       renderer/preload bundling, icon generation
   124	```
   125	
   126	### Why `src/core` is separate
   127	
   128	Everything in `src/core` is pure TypeScript with no Electron, Node or DOM imports.
   129	That is what makes the entire test suite runnable on any machine, and it is why the
   130	dictation state machine (where the subtle bugs live) is fully covered instead of
   131	being assumed correct.
   132	
   133	### Why audio capture happens in a hidden window
   134	
   135	`getUserMedia` and the Web Audio API are browser APIs; Electron does not expose
   136	them to the main process. So a hidden `recorder` window hosts capture, and the main
   137	process asks for it over IPC. The renderer is served from `file://` under a strict
   138	CSP with `contextIsolation` on and `nodeIntegration` off.
   139	
   140	**The preload is deliberately CommonJS.** Electron's preload loader ignores
   141	`"type": "module"`, so an ESM preload must be `.mjs`; a compiled `.js` preload
   142	containing `import` statements fails to load and leaves a dead UI with no visible
   143	error. `scripts/build-renderer.mjs` bundles the preload as CommonJS and asserts
   144	that it did.
   145	
   146	### Where the security boundary is
   147	
   148	The renderer can call 75 named IPC channels and nothing else. It cannot read the
   149	filesystem, and it can never read the API key. It can only ask whether one is
   150	configured. The key is encrypted with DPAPI via Electron's `safeStorage`, so it is
   151	unreadable from another Windows account or from a copy of the file on another
   152	machine.
   153	
   154	---
   155	
   156	## Design decisions worth knowing
   157	
   158	**Delivery never loses your text.** After a dictation the text is either pasted
   159	into your app or left on the clipboard, never neither. Once the Ctrl+V keystroke
   160	has gone out the dictation counts as pasted, and it stays on the clipboard: your
   161	previous clipboard is not put back, because nothing proves the app has taken the
   162	text yet and an app that is slow to respond would paste the old clipboard instead.
   163	If you cancel before the keystroke, your previous clipboard comes back only when
   164	nothing newer was copied meanwhile and it was captured exactly (text, HTML or RTF;
   165	an image or a file list is not). Losing a dictation is worse than losing a clipboard.
   166	
   167	**A silent recording is rejected.** A silent clip can make a speech model echo its
   168	own prompt bias (your dictionary) back as a fake transcript. Clips whose peak
   169	never crosses the speech threshold are refused with an explanation instead.
   170	
   171	**The keyterm list is capped at 400 tokens per request.** Deepgram Nova-3 hard-fails
   172	a request over 500 tokens across all keyterms, which would break *every* dictation
   173	at once. The budget, with a safety margin, is enforced in code and checked by both
   174	a test and the self-test.
   175	
   176	**Edits are never silently discarded.** If a store cannot read its file at launch it
   177	refuses to write, rather than starting empty and overwriting your data on the next
   178	edit. Write failures surface in the UI.
   179	
   180	---
